        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">Waterproofing basement walls from outside</strong> is the only method that keeps the foundation itself dry, and when a block wall is deteriorating or a basement floods repeatedly despite interior measures, it is the correct answer. Budget $15,000-30,000 for a full perimeter and insist on the complete assembly: clean wall, named membrane at a specified thickness, drainage board, new footing drain in washed stone, and free-draining backfill.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Before committing to an excavation, spend a weekend on the cheap fixes — <strong class="text-white">extend the downspouts, clean the gutters, and regrade so water runs away from the house</strong>. A large share of wet basements are solved right there, and even when they are not, those steps cut the load on whatever system you install next.
          </p>
        </div>

